--TEST--
The first use of `Core\Session` with no `[session]` block opens the SQLite store in the data folder, the session it opens reads back what it saved, and `openWithIdentifier` after it throws naming the member that opened it -- `rule:core-api/session-roster`, `rule:core-classes/a-session-opens-on-its-first-use` and `rule:http-server/no-session-block-means-the-data-folder-store`
--GET--
under=a-request
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The case answers a request, because that is the first thing the first use checks
// (`rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`).
//
// No `[session]` block is written, so the store is the data folder's
// `session.sqlite`. The first `get` opens a new, empty session there.
echo "a new session has no value: ", Core\Session::get("visits") == null ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

Core\Session::setShortLived("visits", 1);
echo "the session reads back what it saved: ", Core\Session::get("visits"), "\n";

// The session is open now, so `openWithIdentifier` throws. The message names the
// method that opened it.
uint $refused = 0;
tainted string $said = "";
try {
    Core\Session::openWithIdentifier("PmA5tKz1QvR3sYbNqLwXe2");
} catch (LogicError $twice) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    $said = $twice->message;
}
echo "a second open was refused ", $refused, " of 1\n";
echo "and it names the method that opened the session: ",
    Core\Str::contains($said, "`Core\\Session::get()` opened it") ? "yes" : $said, "\n";
--EXPECT--
a new session has no value: yes
the session reads back what it saved: 1
a second open was refused 1 of 1
and it names the method that opened the session: yes
